# Shared by both images. Docker allows one ignore file per build context, and the
# context here is the repo root for both the backend and the frontend — so this list
# may only exclude what NEITHER image needs. Each Dockerfile then COPYs exactly its
# own inputs, which is what keeps the images small (the previous `COPY . .` relied on
# this file to exclude src/ and package.json, which made the frontend unbuildable).
.git
.gitignore
.venv
node_modules
dist
build
.env
.env.*
.DS_Store
__pycache__
**/__pycache__
*.pyc
.pytest_cache
.vscode
.claude
*.code-workspace
tests
batch
scripts
# ...except the combined image's entrypoint, which must ship inside it.
!scripts/start-combined.sh
*.md
advisor_trace.log
test_data.json
test_verify.json
test_pipeline.py
agent_config.py
data_pipeline.py
# advisor/.news_cache.json IS shipped, deliberately.
#
# Excluding it meant every new revision booted with an empty cache, so the first
# visitor to each family paid a full cold fetch — measured at 33s. Shipping a
# pre-warmed cache makes that first load ~1ms instead: _load_cache_from_disk keeps
# stale entries as well as fresh ones, and get_feed serves a stale feed immediately
# while kicking off a background refresh. The baked contents are only ever the
# starting point, replaced within minutes of boot.
#
# Cost is a 12KB file and a busted layer cache when the file changes. Worth it.
